est move. It gives check to the White king on f1, forcing White to respond immediately. After the king moves (likely Kf1-e2 or Kf1-g2), my bishop remains actively placed and continues to dominate. This forcing move maintains my initiative and keeps White on the defensive. The check also prevents White from organizing any counterplay, which is crucial given that White has an active rook on a3.\n\nAnswer: B: f2-e1"}
