\n1 |    |NWF0|BWF0|QWF0|    |    |NWF0|RWF0| 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[P: a7-a5, P: a7-a6, P: b7-b5, P: b7-b6, N: b8-a6, N: b8-c6, P: c7-c5, P: c7-c6, P: d7-d5, P: d7-d6, P: e5-e4, P: f7-f5, P: f7-f6, R: f8-e8, R: f8-d8, P: g7-g5, P: g7-g6, K: g8-h8, B: h4-g3, B: h4-f2, B: h4-e1, B: h4-g5, B: h4-f6, B: h4-e7, B: h4-d8, P: h7-h5, P: h7-h6]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Situation: I'm playing as Black in a complex middlegame position. White has a rook actively placed on b3 and another on h1, with their king on e2. My king on g8 has castled kingside (with the rook on f8). I have a bishop actively placed on h4, which is deep in White's territory. White's king position looks somewhat exposed on e2. I notice White has several advanced pawns (a4, b4, g4) but also some weaknesses.\n\nEvaluation: Let me consider my most promising moves:\n\n1. **B: h4-f2** - This move puts the bishop on f2, giving check to the White king on e2. This forces White's king to move and maintains pressure. The bishop would be very actively placed, attacking multiple squares and disrupting White's coordination.\n\n2. **B: h4-g3** - Keeps the bishop active but less forcing than f2.\n\n3. **P: e5-e4** - Advances my central pawn, gaining space, but doesn't create immediate threats.\n\n4. **R: f8-e8** - Activates my rook on the e-file where White's king stands, but not as forcing as the bishop check.\n\n5. **N: b8-c6** - Develops the knight but relatively slow given the tactical nature of the position.\n\nReason: **B: h4-f2** is clearly the strongest move. It delivers check to the exposed White king on e2, forcing it to move to a worse square (likely d1, e1, or f1). After the king moves, my bishop on f2 will be superbly placed, controlling key central squares and limiting White's piece coordination. This forcing move maintains the initiative and exploits White's somewhat exposed king position. The check also prevents White from consolidating or launching their own threats.\n\nAnswer: B: h4-f2"}
