ve available. It puts immediate pressure on White's exposed king on e2, forces White to respond to the check (likely Kd2, Kf3, or Kf1), and maintains my bishop's strong position deep in White's territory. This move seizes the initiative and exploits White's king safety issues. In chess, when your opponent's king is vulnerable, forcing moves like checks should be prioritized as they limit your opponent's options and can lead to tactical opportunities.\n\nAnswer: B: f2-e3"}
