acks the queen but allows White more options (queen can move with tempo)\n\nReason: I choose **B: e3-f2** because it gives check to the White king on e2. This forces White's response and disrupts their coordination. After the king moves (likely Kf3, Kd3, or Ke1), I maintain my actively placed bishop and can continue applying pressure. Checks are often the most forcing moves, and in this position, it allows me to dictate the pace of the game while keeping White's king uncomfortable.\n\nAnswer: B: e3-f2"}
