 already-passed pawn, creating immediate threats\n- It restricts White's pieces, particularly limiting the knight's mobility\n- The pawn on e4 controls key central squares (d3, f3)\n- It's forcing and puts pressure on White to respond\n- With White's king on e2, an advanced e-pawn could become a serious asset in the middlegame\n- This move is more forcing than slow development moves\n\nAnswer: P: e5-e4"}
