\n1 |    |    |    |    |    |    |NWF0|RWF0| 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[P: a7-a6, P: b7-b5, P: b7-b6, N: b8-a6, N: b8-c6, P: c7-c5, P: c7-c6, P: d7-d5, P: d7-d6, P: f7-f5, P: f7-f6, R: f8-e8, R: f8-d8, R: f8-g8, R: f8-h8, K: g7-g6, K: g7-g8, K: g7-h8, P: h7-h5, P: h7-h6]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Situation: I'm playing as Black and my king has been moved to g7 (likely due to previous threats). White has an aggressive knight on a5, active rooks on b3 and h1, and their king on c3. My rook on f8 is somewhat active, but my queenside pieces (rook on a8, knight on b8, bishop on c8) are undeveloped. White has advanced pawns and appears to have some central control.\n\nEvaluation: Let me consider several promising moves:\n\n1. **R: f8-e8** - Activates the rook along the e-file, potentially putting pressure on White's position and preparing to contest the center.\n\n2. **N: b8-c6** - Develops the knight to an active square, attacking White's advanced knight on a5 and helping control the center.\n\n3. **P: d7-d5** - Pushes the center pawn, trying to gain space and open lines for my pieces.\n\n4. **R: f8-d8** - Places the rook on the d-file, which could become important if d7-d5 is played later.\n\n5. **P: b7-b5** - Attacks White's knight on a5, forcing it to move and potentially gaining space on the queenside.\n\nReason: The White knight on a5 is somewhat offside and vulnerable. Playing **N: b8-c6** serves multiple purposes:\n- It develops a piece that's been stuck on the back rank\n- It attacks the knight on a5, forcing White to deal with this threat\n- It helps control important central squares (d4, e5)\n- It opens up the possibility of castling queenside in future moves (though my king has moved)\n- It improves my piece coordination\n\nThis move addresses the immediate tactical concern (the knight on a5) while improving my position strategically.\n\nAnswer: N: b8-c6"}
