te's rook at h1 and the pawn at h2. This forces White to respond to the threat of losing material. The knight move is also a classic centralized-to-attacking-position maneuver that increases pressure on White's kingside. White's rook on h1 will have to move, and I can potentially win the h2 pawn or continue applying pressure with my active knight.\n\nAnswer: N: d4-f3"}
